=== L1 Factors to Consider Before Training ===
id: 89effb02-62e0-4a7f-92e4-881b47b014a2
slug: lesson-01
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:40:29.074217+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 24

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Pre-training essentials: PAR-Q, SMART goals, current fitness, age, lifestyle, cost and available facilities.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Why Planning Ahead Matters</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Before a coach writes a single session plan, they must gather information about the performer they are working with. Jumping straight into training without this groundwork is ineffective at best and dangerous at worst. A training programme that ignores a performer's medical history, current fitness level, lifestyle or available resources will fail to produce results — and may even cause harm. The factors to consider fall into two broad groups: <strong>personal factors</strong> (relating to the individual) and <strong>environmental factors</strong> (relating to the setting and resources available).</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Health Screening and the PAR-Q</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">The first step in any new training programme is a <dfn class="term" data-def="Physical Activity Readiness Questionnaire — a short self-report health screening tool used to identify any medical conditions or risk factors that could make exercise unsafe.">PAR-Q</dfn> (Physical Activity Readiness Questionnaire). This is a short questionnaire that asks about symptoms such as chest pain, dizziness, bone or joint problems, and whether a doctor has ever advised against vigorous exercise. A 'yes' answer to any question does not automatically prevent someone from training, but it flags that medical clearance should be sought before they begin. The PAR-Q protects both the performer and the coach: if a participant suffers an adverse event during training and no health screening was completed, the coach may face professional or legal consequences. Health screening more broadly includes checking for <strong>medical conditions</strong> such as asthma, diabetes or a known heart condition, each of which will influence the type, intensity and duration of training that is appropriate.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n5" data-revision-tip="Remember PAR-Q stands for Physical Activity Readiness Questionnaire. In an exam you may be asked what it is, why it is used or what a coach should do if a performer answers 'yes' to a question.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The PAR-Q is completed before starting any new training programme. It screens for medical conditions and cardiovascular risk factors. A 'yes' answer means the performer should seek medical advice before training begins.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n6">Current Fitness Level and Test Results</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n7">Once health screening is complete, the coach needs to establish a <strong>baseline</strong> — a starting point from which progress can be measured. This means carrying out appropriate fitness tests (such as the multi-stage fitness test for cardiovascular endurance or the sit-and-reach for flexibility) and recording the results. Knowing the performer's current fitness level allows the coach to set training at an appropriate intensity — too easy and no adaptation will occur; too hard and the performer risks injury or drop-out. Test results also highlight specific strengths and weaknesses, so the programme can target the components of fitness most relevant to the performer's sport.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n8">Goals and SMART Targets</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n9">Understanding what the performer wants to achieve is essential before any programme is written. Goals should be set using the <dfn class="term" data-def="A framework for setting goals that are Specific, Measurable, Agreed/Achievable, Realistic and Time-phased, ensuring they are clear, motivating and trackable.">SMART</dfn> framework: <strong>Specific</strong> (clearly defined, e.g. 'improve 10 km run time'), <strong>Measurable</strong> (tracked with a number or test result), <strong>Agreed/Achievable</strong> (agreed between coach and performer, and within reach), <strong>Realistic</strong> (attainable given the performer's current level and resources) and <strong>Time-phased</strong> (with a clear deadline). SMART targets improve exercise adherence — performers who have a clear goal are more motivated and more likely to stick to the programme. Setting both short-term targets (e.g. improve press-up score by 10 in six weeks) and a longer-term goal (e.g. complete a 10 km race in under 50 minutes by the end of term) helps maintain focus and momentum throughout training.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n10">Personal Factors: Age, Gender, Lifestyle and Time Available</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n11">Beyond health and goals, the coach must consider a range of personal factors. <strong>Age</strong> affects the type of training that is safe and effective: younger athletes with developing bodies may need to avoid heavy resistance work, while older performers have reduced muscle elasticity and need more recovery time between sessions. <strong>Gender</strong> influences physiological differences in average strength, hormonal profile and some injury susceptibilities, and should inform exercise selection and intensity guidance. <strong>Lifestyle</strong> — covering factors such as diet, sleep patterns, work or school commitments, stress levels and social habits — determines how much the performer can recover between sessions. A performer who sleeps poorly, eats erratically and has a physically demanding job will need a lighter training load than someone with a healthy routine and plenty of rest. <strong>Time available for training</strong> and the <strong>cost of training</strong> are practical limits: the programme must be realistic about how many sessions per week a performer can commit to, and what resources they can afford.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n12" data-revision-tip="Personal factors are: health, fitness, age, gender, lifestyle, time available for training and cost of training. Learn all seven — short-answer questions often ask you to 'state two personal factors'.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Personal factors include health, fitness, age, gender, lifestyle, time available for training and cost of training. Each one can limit or shape what a training programme looks like in practice.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n13">Environmental Factors: Facilities, Equipment and the Sport's Demands</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n14">Even a perfectly designed programme is useless if the performer cannot access the facilities or equipment needed to carry it out. <dfn class="term" data-def="The physical spaces, venues and equipment available to a performer that make certain types of training possible or restrict what can be included in a programme.">Environmental factors</dfn> include the availability of a swimming pool, gym, track, sports hall or open space, and the equipment (resistance machines, free weights, agility ladders, etc.) that the performer can use. The coach must also analyse the <strong>demands of the sport</strong>: a swimmer needs cardiovascular endurance and muscular endurance training, whereas a sprinter needs power, speed and explosive strength work. Designing a programme without analysing what the sport actually requires means training the wrong components.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n15">Safety, Injury Risk and Warm-Up/Cool-Down</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n16">Safety must be considered at every stage of programme design. Before training begins, the coach should carry out a <strong>risk assessment</strong> — checking facilities, equipment, participant numbers and the environment for hazards. A performer with a known vulnerability (a previous knee injury, for example) needs a programme that avoids aggravating that area. <dfn class="term" data-def="A structured preparatory phase at the start of a session that raises pulse and body temperature, improves muscle elasticity and prepares the performer mentally for exercise.">Warm-up</dfn> and cool-down must be built into every session: a warm-up raises heart rate and temperature, increases blood flow to muscles and reduces injury risk, while a <strong>cool-down</strong> gradually lowers heart rate, removes metabolic waste products from working muscles and aids recovery. Skipping these components significantly increases both acute and overuse injury risk.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>What does a risk assessment involve before training?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n17">A risk assessment before a training session involves four key checks: facilities (are surfaces safe, is space adequate, is the venue free from trip hazards?), equipment (is it in good repair, correctly adjusted and appropriate for the participants?), participants (are there any medical conditions, previous injuries or fitness concerns that change what is safe for this group?) and rules or laws (does the activity comply with health and safety regulations and the sport's governing body guidelines?). A written risk assessment protects participants and demonstrates the coach's duty of care. Even for informal training sessions, mentally running through these checks before starting is good professional practice.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Why do cost and time constraints matter when designing a programme?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n18">A training programme that a performer cannot sustain financially or logistically will fail, regardless of its scientific soundness. If a coach prescribes five gym sessions per week but the performer can only afford two visits and has school commitments every evening, adherence will collapse. Cost of training covers gym membership fees, equipment purchases, travel, specialist coaching and competition entry fees. Time available must account for school or work, family responsibilities, travel to training venues and recovery time. The best coaches design programmes that fit the performer's real life — not an idealised schedule. A realistic but consistent programme will always outperform a perfect programme that is abandoned after two weeks.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n20">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">Before designing a training programme, a coach must consider personal factors (health, fitness, age, gender, lifestyle, time available and cost) and environmental factors (facilities and equipment available).</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">The PAR-Q is a health screening questionnaire completed before any new programme begins; a 'yes' answer means the performer should seek medical advice first.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">Goals must be set using the SMART framework (Specific, Measurable, Agreed/Achievable, Realistic, Time-phased) to improve motivation and exercise adherence.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n24">Safety considerations include a risk assessment, knowledge of injury history and medical conditions, and building warm-up and cool-down into every session.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n19">In short-answer questions, the examiner will often give you a scenario — for example, a 16-year-old with asthma who wants to get fitter for football but can only train twice a week at school. Your job is to identify and apply the relevant factors. Name each factor precisely (use the correct terms — 'personal factors', 'PAR-Q', 'SMART targets', 'environmental factors'), then explain why it matters for that specific performer. In extended-answer questions worth 6 or 8 marks, show that factors interact: for instance, the performer's age and medical condition (asthma) together limit the intensity that is safe, which must then be reflected in the SMART target that is set. Answers that treat each factor in isolation typically fall in the Developing band; answers that show how factors connect reach Mastering.</p>

