=== L2 The Principles of Training ===
id: 82f2b97c-3ea6-4e54-9289-9a4ba4b576f1
slug: lesson-02
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:39:30.525934+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 27

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Specificity, overload, reversibility, variance and individual needs; how FITT helps coaches design effective programmes.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Why Principles Matter</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Any training programme can make an athlete fitter — for a while. But without a structured framework, progress stalls, performance peaks prematurely, or the athlete risks injury through poorly judged workloads. The <dfn class="term" data-def="A set of guidelines that underpin the design of any effective training programme, ensuring it produces real, lasting improvements in fitness.">principles of training</dfn> give coaches a shared language and a set of rules to design programmes that produce genuine, lasting gains. Each principle addresses a different risk: specificity guards against wasted effort, overload drives adaptation, reversibility reminds us that fitness is temporary, variance keeps athletes engaged, and individual needs ensure the programme fits the person, not just the sport.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Specificity</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4"><dfn class="term" data-def="Training must be relevant to the demands of the sport and the athlete's specific role within it, so that adaptations occur in the right muscles, energy systems and movement patterns.">Specificity</dfn> states that the body adapts specifically to the type of stress placed upon it. A long-distance runner who only does weight training will develop muscular strength but will not improve cardiovascular endurance — they have trained the wrong system. A coach applying specificity asks: What energy systems does this sport primarily use? What muscle groups are most important? What movement patterns must be replicated? A basketball coach, for example, would design drills involving explosive lateral movements, short sprints and jumping — not long, steady-state running — because those match the physiological and technical demands of the game. Specificity also applies to position: a goalkeeper's training programme will differ markedly from a midfielder's even within the same sport.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Progressive Overload</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">The body adapts to a given training load and then reaches a plateau. To keep improving, the load must be progressively increased — this is <dfn class="term" data-def="Gradually increasing the demands of training over time so that the body is continually challenged and forced to adapt beyond its current level of fitness.">progressive overload</dfn>. Overload means working at a level above what the body is accustomed to; progressive means the increase is gradual and planned rather than sudden. A swimmer who completes 20 lengths per session without discomfort must eventually swim 22, then 24 lengths to continue improving. If the overload is too rapid, the risk of injury rises sharply; if it is absent, fitness stagnates. Progressive overload is the engine of fitness improvement and is closely linked to the FITT principle, which gives coaches four specific handles to control the overload applied.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n7" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall all four components of the FITT principle with a sporting example for each. This is frequently tested as a four-mark question.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The FITT principle provides four variables a coach can manipulate to apply progressive overload: Frequency (how often), Intensity (how hard), Time (how long), and Type (which training method). Increasing any one of these variables increases the training load on the athlete's body.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n8">The FITT Principle</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n9">The <dfn class="term" data-def="A framework for applying progressive overload by adjusting four variables: Frequency, Intensity, Time and Type of training.">FITT principle</dfn> gives a coach four specific levers to increase training load in a controlled way. <strong>Frequency</strong> is how often an athlete trains — moving from three sessions a week to four increases the total weekly training stimulus. <strong>Intensity</strong> is how hard the athlete works — a runner increasing their average pace from 8 km/h to 9 km/h imposes a greater cardiovascular demand. <strong>Time</strong> refers to the duration of each session — extending a session from 30 minutes to 45 minutes adds volume. <strong>Type</strong> is the training method selected — switching from continuous running to interval training recruits the anaerobic energy system more heavily and creates a different physiological adaptation. Coaches rarely change all four variables simultaneously; instead, they adjust one or two each week or training block to ensure overload is applied safely and systematically.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n10">Reversibility</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n11">Fitness is not permanent. When training stops or is significantly reduced, the body reverses its adaptations — cardiovascular endurance declines, muscle mass decreases and flexibility is lost. This is the principle of <dfn class="term" data-def="The loss of fitness adaptations that occurs when training is reduced or stopped entirely, as the body no longer needs to maintain the higher level of conditioning.">reversibility</dfn>, sometimes described with the phrase "use it or lose it". Research suggests cardiovascular fitness begins to decline noticeably within two to three weeks of inactivity, while strength losses are slightly slower but still significant within four to six weeks. A coach managing a swimmer recovering from illness must plan a structured return to training, gradually rebuilding the load rather than expecting the athlete to resume immediately at their previous level. Reversibility also underlines the importance of rest and recovery being planned — complete rest for weeks at a time is rarely beneficial.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n12" data-revision-tip="Remember: reversibility is the principle that explains why fitness declines during injury lay-offs, illness or unplanned rest periods. It is the opposite process to progressive overload.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Reversibility means that fitness gains built through months of training can be lost within weeks if training stops. Cardiovascular endurance declines fastest — noticeable changes occur within two to three weeks — while strength takes slightly longer to reverse.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n13">Variance (Tedium)</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n14">Repeating exactly the same sessions week after week causes two problems: physical plateauing, as the body adapts completely to a familiar stimulus, and psychological tedium, as athletes lose motivation through repetition. The principle of variance — sometimes called the principle of tedium — requires coaches to introduce variety into training to prevent both. A football coach might rotate between circuit training, fartlek running, small-sided games and plyometric sessions during a pre-season block. Varying the type of training also ensures that multiple components of fitness are developed — not just the one or two that appear in the regular session format. Importantly, variance should not mean randomness; sessions should still follow specificity and progressive overload principles — variety is introduced within a structured plan.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n15">Individual Needs</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n16">No two athletes are identical. Their current fitness levels, injury history, age, gender, role in the sport, goals and available training time all differ. The principle of individual needs states that a training programme must be tailored to the specific performer, not applied generically. A 16-year-old beginner taking up rugby will require a very different programme from a 22-year-old experienced player preparing for a county trial — even if both play the same position. A coach who ignores individual needs risks under-challenging a fit athlete (who will plateau and become bored) or over-loading a less-fit one (who risks injury and burnout). Fitness testing data — gathered from tests such as the multistage fitness test or the Illinois agility run — gives a coach the objective baseline they need to set genuinely individual training loads.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n17">Rest, Recovery and Applying the Principles</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n18">Progressive overload works only if adequate rest is built into the programme. During exercise, muscle fibres are stressed and micro-damaged; it is during recovery that the body repairs and strengthens these fibres, producing the adaptation that improves fitness. Without sufficient rest, the cumulative damage exceeds the body's repair capacity, leading to overtraining, fatigue and injury. A coach designing a weekly programme for a 100-metre sprinter might schedule three high-intensity sessions with rest or light activity days between them, allowing full neuromuscular recovery before the next intense stimulus. Rest is not wasted training time — it is when the adaptations triggered by overload actually take place. A well-designed programme applies all five principles together: it is specific to the sport, progressively overloaded via FITT, varied to maintain motivation, and tailored to the individual — with rest built in at every stage.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>How does a coach apply FITT to a practical training plan?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n19">Imagine a coach preparing a recreational cyclist for a 10-week sportive ride. In week one, the athlete trains three times a week (frequency), at a moderate steady pace (intensity), for 40 minutes per session (time), using continuous cycling (type). By week six the coach has increased frequency to four sessions, pushed intensity into threshold pace for intervals, extended two sessions to 60 minutes, and introduced interval training to target the aerobic-anaerobic boundary. Each change is deliberate — only one or two FITT variables change in any given training block, so the coach can identify what is driving improvement and avoid over-loading the athlete. This systematic adjustment is exactly what the exam expects candidates to be able to describe and apply to a given scenario.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>What happens if overload is applied too quickly?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n20">When training load is increased too sharply — more than roughly 10% per week as a general guideline — the cumulative stress on muscles, tendons and bones exceeds the body's ability to repair itself. The result is overtraining syndrome: persistent fatigue, declining performance, irritability, suppressed immune function and a significantly elevated injury risk. Common overtraining injuries include stress fractures, tendinopathy and muscle strains. This is why progressive overload must be gradual and why rest days are non-negotiable components of any well-designed programme. A good coach monitors signs of overtraining (flat performance, low mood, disturbed sleep) and is prepared to reduce load — temporarily applying the principle of reversibility deliberately to allow full recovery.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n22">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">Specificity means training must match the demands of the sport and the athlete's role — the right energy systems, muscles and movement patterns must be targeted.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n24">Progressive overload drives adaptation: the FITT principle (Frequency, Intensity, Time, Type) gives coaches four variables to increase training load in a controlled, systematic way.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n25">Reversibility reminds coaches that fitness is temporary — two to three weeks of inactivity causes measurable cardiovascular decline, so return-to-training plans must rebuild load gradually.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n26">Variance prevents physical plateaus and psychological tedium by rotating training methods, while individual needs ensures the programme is tailored to the specific athlete rather than applied generically.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n27">Rest and recovery are not optional extras — adaptation occurs during recovery, and insufficient rest leads to overtraining, declining performance and increased injury risk.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n21">In applied-scenario questions, always link the principle to the specific sport or performer described. Writing "the coach should use progressive overload" scores 1 mark; writing "the coach should gradually increase the number of interval reps each week to ensure the sprinter's anaerobic system is continually challenged" scores 2 or 3 marks. For FITT questions, name all four components, define each briefly, then give a sport-specific example of how each could be increased. In extended responses, show that the principles work together — specificity directs what to overload, FITT controls how to overload it, variance prevents plateauing, and individual needs determines the starting point for each athlete.</p>

